Policy ID: DISC-001
Category: Disclaimer
Title: Business Communication Disclaimer

Definition:
A violation occurs when sensitive financial, commercial, transactional, client-related, or strategic business information is distributed, prepared for distribution, or proposed for distribution without a required disclaimer or distribution restriction.

Violations:
- Distributing sensitive financial information without the required disclaimer
- Distributing sensitive commercial information without the required disclaimer
- Distributing sensitive transaction or client information without the required restriction
- Preparing sensitive business information for distribution without the required disclaimer
- Proposing distribution of sensitive information without the required disclaimer

Exceptions:
- Required disclaimer is already present
- Authorized distribution
- Required distribution restriction is already present
- Information is outside the scope of the disclaimer requirement
- Distribution is explicitly authorized without a disclaimer

Examples:
- Sensitive financial briefing proposed for distribution without the required disclaimer
- Sensitive commercial information prepared for distribution without the required restriction
- Sensitive business information proposed for distribution to external recipients



Policy ID: SEC-001
Category: Secrecy
Title: Confidential Information and Unauthorized Disclosure

Definition:
A violation occurs when confidential, restricted, proprietary, or material non-public information is disclosed, shared, forwarded, accessed, or provided to an unauthorized person.

Violations:
- Disclosing confidential information to an unauthorized recipient
- Sharing restricted or proprietary information without authorization
- Forwarding protected information to an unauthorized person
- Providing unauthorized access to protected information
- Requesting or encouraging unauthorized disclosure

Exceptions:
- Authorized disclosure
- Authorized recipient
- Legitimate confidentiality agreement
- Instruction to keep information confidential without actually disclosing it
- Legitimate business communication that does not reveal protected information

Examples:
- Sending confidential information to an unauthorized recipient
- Forwarding restricted business information without authorization
- Telling someone not to disclose an agreement until it is signed is not itself a secrecy violation



Policy ID: ETH-001
Category: Employee ethics
Title: Employee Ethical Conduct

Definition:
A violation occurs when official company communication or workplace activity is used for inappropriate personal or social conduct, dishonest conduct, deceptive conduct, improper workplace behavior, or intentional falsification or misrepresentation of business information.

Violations:
- Using official company communication for inappropriate personal or social activity
- Discussing personal drinking or drinks unrelated to legitimate business activity through official communication
- Using company communication for inappropriate personal arrangements
- Engaging in dishonest or deceptive business conduct
- Intentionally changing figures to make results appear more favorable
- Falsifying or misrepresenting business information
- Concealing information for an improper purpose
- Bypassing required workplace controls or review processes

Exceptions:
- Legitimate business communication
- Authorized company event
- Authorized business entertainment
- Legitimate business hospitality
- Accurate correction of information
- Clearly personal communication outside official business channels

Examples:
- Discussing personal drinks through official company email
- Discussing personal social activities through official business communication
- Adjusting figures so results look more favorable
- Changing a report to create a misleading impression
- Proceeding without the usual compliance review



Policy ID: COM-001
Category: Change in communication
Title: Unauthorized Communication Channels

Definition:
A violation occurs when company business communication is moved, redirected, or continued through an unauthorized personal or external communication channel.

Violations:
- Moving business communication to a personal channel
- Continuing company business through an unauthorized messaging service
- Using personal email or accounts for company business
- Redirecting business communication to an external channel
- Inviting a business contact to continue work-related communication outside the approved channel

Exceptions:
- Explicitly authorized external channel
- Clearly personal communication unrelated to business
- Company-approved communication method
- Business process specifically requiring the external channel

Examples:
- Continuing a business discussion through WhatsApp
- Continuing business communication through a personal account
- Moving a company discussion to a personal messaging service


